From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 01:28:59 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] mesa/st: store integer pixel transfers without a blit

A pixel transfer saturates an integer into the destination range. The blit
paths hand the texels to a colour attachment, which keeps only the
destination's low bits, so a conversion between two different pure-integer
formats loses that saturation. ReadPixels already refused the signed/unsigned
half of it. Report the conversion from the formats instead, so narrowing and
component changes are covered too, and refuse it in the texture upload,
GetTexImage, and pixel-buffer paths that had no such check.

Conversions through a floating-point or normalized format, and transfers whose
source and destination formats match, keep their blit. Check that the ones
still routed through a blit are exactly the ones the generated blit shader
reproduces.

Routing those reads past the blit sends them to the ReadPixels fallback, whose
compute download named slice 0 of the read attachment's texture instead of the
slice the attachment is bound to. Every read of a layered attachment therefore
returned layer 0 once the region was large enough for the driver to prefer the
compute download over the CPU copy, so the OpenGL CTS reported layer 0's
contents for every other layer of a 320x64x8 R32 image:
dEQP-GL45-ES31.functional.image_load_store.{2d_array,3d}.atomic.*_return_value.
Cube faces were unaffected because their base layer already selects the face.
Name the attachment's slice so the download reads it.

An array slice reaches the download through the sampler view, but its
conversion shader samples a 3D texture at the invocation's own z and has no
depth offset to start from, so it silently dropped the one GetTexSubImage
already passed it. Refuse a 3D download that starts away from the first slice
and let the CPU copy, which honours it, take those reads.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
